/-!
# Assembling a Tate resolution from a prescribed boundary

A `Resolution` has twenty-one fields. Eighteen of them are supplied by the graded divided-power
polynomial algebra on a prescribed family of variables together with a prescribed boundary for
each variable: the algebra, its grading, its strict graded commutativity, its divided powers,
its coefficient map and its presentation come from `IndexedPresentation`; freeness of every
homogeneous piece from `IndexedPresentation.free_grade`; the differential is
`IndexedPresentation.chosenDifferential` and the two compatibility fields come from
`isDifferentialGradedAlgebra_of_differential`; the augmentation and its three fields from
`exists_augmentation`; and the degree-zero field from
`mem_grade_zero_iff_exists_coefficient`.

The remaining three are `augmentation_differential`, `exact_zero` and `exact_positive`, and
they constrain the prescribed boundary rather than the algebra. This file collects them, with
the condition that the prescribed boundaries be cycles — which is what makes the algebra a
differential graded algebra at all — into one predicate `IsResolvingBoundary`, and shows that a
boundary satisfying it assembles a resolution. So the whole of [Stacks, Tag 09PN] is reduced to
exhibiting such a boundary.

## Implementation notes

The differential is named, not quantified over. **The same is not true of the differential** as
of the augmentation: the differential is only a linear map, and adding to it any linear map
vanishing on the coefficients and on the variables leaves those values unchanged while changing
which cycles are boundaries and whether the augmentation kills every boundary. So a predicate
quantifying over every linear map with the prescribed values asserts the three fields for maps
that are not differentials at all. That is why the differential here is
`IndexedPresentation.chosenDifferential`, a function of the presentation and the boundary.

The augmentation *is* quantified over, but by three conditions and not two: its values on the
coefficients, its values on the variables, and its vanishing in positive degrees. The first two
do not determine it. This algebra is generated by the coefficients and the variables together
with the *divided powers* of the variables of positive even degree, and a divided power is not
in the algebra they generate without it: `k ! * γ k x = x ^ k`, so an algebra map killing `x`
makes `γ k x` go to a `k !`-torsion element, not necessarily to zero.
`IndexedPresentation.existsUnique_hom` says as much — it asserts uniqueness only among maps that
are also graded and compatible with the divided powers. With two conditions the predicate would
assert `augmentation_differential` of maps that do not satisfy it, since a boundary of degree
one or more is killed only by a map vanishing in positive degrees; with three it is exactly what
`exists_augmentation` supplies.

## Main definitions

* `DividedPowers.TateResolution.IsResolvingBoundary` — the conditions on a prescribed boundary
  that the three remaining fields of a resolution ask for.

## Main results

* `DividedPowers.TateResolution.exists_resolution_of_isResolvingBoundary` — a resolving boundary
  assembles a Tate resolution, with finitely many variables in each degree when the prescribed
  family is finite in each degree.
* `DividedPowers.TateResolution.exists_finite_tateResolution_of_isResolvingBoundary` — the
  finite assertion of [Stacks, Tag 09PN], reduced to exhibiting a resolving boundary over a
  finite polynomial presentation. The presentation itself is supplied here, by
  `exists_finite_polynomial_presentation`, and the resolving boundary is asked for only over the
  *finite* index sets that supplies — which is what makes the coefficient algebra Noetherian.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 09PN](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09PN)
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace DividedPowers.TateResolution

universe u v w

/-- The conditions on a prescribed boundary `t` of the variables of `T` under which `T` is a
Tate resolution of `S` along `φ`: the boundaries are cycles, the augmentation kills every
boundary, and every cycle of nonnegative degree killed by the augmentation is a boundary.

The differential is the one `t` prescribes, `IndexedPresentation.chosenDifferential`. The
augmentation is quantified over, described by its values on the coefficients and on the
variables **and by its vanishing in positive degrees**, because that is how
`exists_augmentation` produces it. All three are needed: the values alone do *not* determine an
algebra map out of this algebra, which is generated by the coefficients and the variables
*together with their divided powers*, and `IndexedPresentation.existsUnique_hom` accordingly
asserts uniqueness only among maps that are graded and compatible with the divided powers. A
map with the prescribed values but not vanishing in positive degrees need not kill a boundary of
degree one or more, so without the third condition the predicate asserts
`augmentation_differential` of maps for which it is false. -/
public def IsResolvingBoundary {R : Type u} {S : Type v} {σ : Type w}
    [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [Algebra R S] (φ : MvPolynomial σ R →ₐ[R] S)
    {idx : ℕ → Type (max u v w)}
    (T : IndexedPresentation.{u, max u w, v} R (MvPolynomial σ R) idx)
    (t : (Σ n : ℕ, idx n) → T.A) (ht : ∀ j, t j ∈ T.grade j.1) : Prop :=
  (∀ j : Σ n : ℕ, idx n, T.chosenDifferential t ht (t j) = 0) ∧
  ∀ a : T.A →ₐ[R] S,
    (∀ p : MvPolynomial σ R, a (T.coefficient p) = φ p) →
    (∀ j : Σ n : ℕ, idx n, a (T.gen j) = 0) →
    (∀ (n : ℕ) (x : T.A), 0 < n → x ∈ T.grade n → a x = 0) →
    (∀ x : T.A, a (T.chosenDifferential t ht x) = 0) ∧
    (∀ x : T.A, x ∈ T.grade 0 → a x = 0 →
      ∃ y : T.A, y ∈ T.grade 1 ∧ T.chosenDifferential t ht y = x) ∧
    (∀ (n : ℕ) (x : T.A), 0 < n → x ∈ T.grade n → T.chosenDifferential t ht x = 0 →
      ∃ y : T.A, y ∈ T.grade (n + 1) ∧ T.chosenDifferential t ht y = x)

/-- **A resolving boundary assembles a Tate resolution.** Every field of `Resolution` other than
`augmentation_differential`, `exact_zero` and `exact_positive` is one call to something the
prescribed family of variables already provides; those three are `IsResolvingBoundary`. -/
public theorem exists_resolution_of_isResolvingBoundary {R : Type u} {S : Type v} {σ : Type w}
    [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [Algebra R S] (φ : MvPolynomial σ R →ₐ[R] S)
    (hφ : Function.Surjective φ) {idx : ℕ → Type (max u v w)}
    (T : IndexedPresentation.{u, max u w, v} R (MvPolynomial σ R) idx)
    (hfin : ∀ n : ℕ, Finite (idx n))
    (t : (Σ n : ℕ, idx n) → T.A) (ht : ∀ j, t j ∈ T.grade j.1)
    (hres : IsResolvingBoundary φ T t ht) :
    ∃ T' : Resolution R S σ φ,
      letI : Ring T'.A := T'.ring
      letI : Algebra R T'.A := T'.algebra
      letI : GradedAlgebra T'.grade := T'.graded
      letI : IsStrictlyGradedComm T'.grade := T'.strict
      ∀ n : ℕ, Finite (T'.polynomial.indices n) := by
  obtain ⟨hcyc, hres2⟩ := hres
  obtain ⟨a, ha1, ha2, ha3, ha4⟩ :=
    exists_augmentation T.grade T.gamma T.coefficient T.polynomial φ
  obtain ⟨haugD, hex0, hexp⟩ := hres2 a ha1 (fun j => ha2 (T.indexEquiv j)) ha3
  obtain ⟨hdga, hcomp⟩ :=
    isDifferentialGradedAlgebra_of_differential T.grade T.gamma T.coefficient
      (T.chosenDifferential t ht) T.polynomial (T.chosenDifferential_coefficient t ht)
      (T.chosenDifferential_grade_zero t ht) (T.chosenDifferential_mem_grade t ht)
      (T.chosenDifferential_leibniz t ht) (T.chosenDifferential_dpow t ht)
      (fun j => by
        rw [show T.polynomial.generator j = T.gen (T.indexEquiv.symm j) from
            (congrArg T.polynomial.generator (T.indexEquiv.apply_symm_apply j)).symm,
          T.chosenDifferential_gen t ht]
        exact hcyc _)
  refine ⟨{ A := T.A
            ring := T.ring
            algebra := T.algebra
            grade := T.grade
            graded := T.graded
            free := T.free_grade hfin
            strict := T.strict
            gamma := T.gamma
            differential := T.chosenDifferential t ht
            dga := hdga
            compatible := hcomp
            coefficient := T.coefficient
            augmentation := a
            polynomial := T.polynomial
            augmentation_surjective := ha4 hφ
            degree_zero := mem_grade_zero_iff_exists_coefficient T.polynomial
            augmentation_coefficient := AlgHom.ext ha1
            augmentation_positive := ha3
            augmentation_differential := haugD
            exact_zero := hex0
            exact_positive := hexp }, ?_⟩
  exact T.finite_indices hfin

/-- **The finite assertion of [Stacks, Tag 09PN], reduced to exhibiting a resolving boundary.**
The finite polynomial presentation of the degree-zero part is supplied here; what remains is a
family of variables, finite in each degree, and a boundary on it satisfying
`IsResolvingBoundary`, over a finite set of degree-zero variables. The finiteness of that set is
passed to the hypothesis rather than discarded: `MvPolynomial σ R` is Noetherian only for finite
`σ`, and the count of variables a degree-by-degree construction adjoins rests on that. -/
public theorem exists_finite_tateResolution_of_isResolvingBoundary {R : Type u} {S : Type v}
    [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [Algebra R S] [Algebra.FiniteType R S]
    (H : ∀ (σ : Type (max u v)), Finite σ →
      ∀ φ : MvPolynomial σ R →ₐ[R] S, Function.Surjective φ →
      ∃ (idx : ℕ → Type (max u v))
        (T : IndexedPresentation.{u, max u v, v} R (MvPolynomial σ R) idx)
        (t : (Σ n : ℕ, idx n) → T.A) (ht : ∀ j, t j ∈ T.grade j.1),
        (∀ n : ℕ, Finite (idx n)) ∧ IsResolvingBoundary φ T t ht) :
    ∃ (σ : Type (max u v)) (φ : MvPolynomial σ R →ₐ[R] S) (_hφ : Function.Surjective φ)
      (T : Resolution R S σ φ),
      letI : Ring T.A := T.ring
      letI : Algebra R T.A := T.algebra
      letI : GradedAlgebra T.grade := T.graded
      letI : IsStrictlyGradedComm T.grade := T.strict
      Finite σ ∧ (∀ n : ℕ, Finite (T.polynomial.indices n)) := by
  obtain ⟨σ, hσ, φ, hφ⟩ := exists_finite_polynomial_presentation R S
  obtain ⟨idx, T, t, ht, hfin, hres⟩ := H σ hσ φ hφ
  obtain ⟨T', hT'⟩ := exists_resolution_of_isResolvingBoundary φ hφ T hfin t ht hres
  exact ⟨σ, φ, hφ, T', hσ, hT'⟩

end DividedPowers.TateResolution

end
